1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,940
Some nights never end.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,878
One explosion. And an entire city,

3
00:00:05,898 --> 00:00:06,980
emptied forever.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,260
The men who fought the fire never came home.

5
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,680
This is the story of Chernobyl.

6
00:00:17,195 --> 00:00:19,962
April twenty-sixth, nineteen eighty-six.

7
00:00:19,982 --> 00:00:21,655
Pripyat, Soviet Ukraine.

8
00:00:22,278 --> 00:00:24,466
Nearby, at the Chernobyl nuclear plant,

9
00:00:24,486 --> 00:00:25,618
a test was underway.

10
00:00:28,195 --> 00:00:29,515
One twenty-three a.m.

11
00:00:33,361 --> 00:00:34,758
The reactor had exploded.

12
00:00:34,778 --> 00:00:36,761
Its core burned under the open sky.

13
00:00:37,695 --> 00:00:39,480
Firefighters arrived within minutes.

14
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:41,555
They had no idea what they were fighting.

15
00:00:42,361 --> 00:00:44,602
No protective suits. No warnings.

16
00:00:44,622 --> 00:00:45,581
Just the fire.

17
00:00:46,361 --> 00:00:48,221
Many of them died within weeks.

18
00:00:49,361 --> 00:00:51,601
And Pripyat was still fast asleep.

19
00:00:52,695 --> 00:00:55,622
This Ferris wheel was due to open on the first of May.

20
00:00:55,642 --> 00:00:56,542
It never turned.

21
00:01:03,161 --> 00:01:04,437
But how did it happen?

22
00:01:04,457 --> 00:01:05,910
That night, at Unit Four,

23
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:07,461
a safety test was running.

24
00:01:09,361 --> 00:01:11,287
The question was whether the turbine

25
00:01:11,307 --> 00:01:12,800
could keep the pumps running

26
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:14,441
for a while after a power cut.

27
00:01:16,061 --> 00:01:17,282
The test was delayed.

28
00:01:17,302 --> 00:01:19,961
For hours, the reactor ran at very low power.

29
00:01:21,561 --> 00:01:22,391
In that state,

30
00:01:22,411 --> 00:01:24,901
this reactor became dangerously unstable.

31
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:31,081
At one twenty-three a.m., the test began.

32
00:01:33,661 --> 00:01:34,607
Seconds later,

33
00:01:34,627 --> 00:01:37,661
the emergency button, A Z five, was pressed.

34
00:01:40,661 --> 00:01:42,602
But a design flaw meant the power,

35
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:45,101
instead of falling, surged many times over.

36
00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:48,981
And within seconds, the reactor exploded.

37
00:01:53,195 --> 00:01:54,026
Morning came.

38
00:01:54,046 --> 00:01:56,535
A column of smoke rose on the horizon.

39
00:01:57,861 --> 00:01:59,581
But nobody was told anything.

40
00:02:00,361 --> 00:02:01,251
It was Saturday.

41
00:02:01,271 --> 00:02:03,810
Children went to school, couples got married.

42
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:05,081
Life went on as usual.

43
00:02:06,028 --> 00:02:07,854
Meanwhile, the reactor burned.

44
00:02:07,874 --> 00:02:10,888
Helicopters began dropping sand and lead into it.

45
00:02:11,695 --> 00:02:13,033
Thirty-six hours later,

46
00:02:13,053 --> 00:02:15,100
loudspeakers made the announcement.

47
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:16,655
The whole city must leave.

48
00:02:19,195 --> 00:02:20,120
Outside the city,

49
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:21,975
a long line of buses was waiting.

50
00:02:22,611 --> 00:02:23,574
People were told

51
00:02:23,594 --> 00:02:25,991
to bring only essentials and documents.

52
00:02:26,611 --> 00:02:27,960
Fifty thousand people,

53
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:29,411
three and a half hours.

54
00:02:29,771 --> 00:02:31,951
They thought it would only be a few days.

55
00:02:32,861 --> 00:02:33,858
By that evening,

56
00:02:33,878 --> 00:02:35,721
Pripyat was completely empty.

57
00:02:43,161 --> 00:02:45,302
On the morning of April twenty-eighth,

58
00:02:45,322 --> 00:02:47,881
alarms went off at a nuclear plant in Sweden.

59
00:02:49,361 --> 00:02:50,244
Radioactive dust

60
00:02:50,264 --> 00:02:52,181
was found on the workers' clothes.

61
00:02:56,061 --> 00:02:56,680
Tests showed

62
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,041
the radiation did not come from their plant.

63
00:03:01,561 --> 00:03:03,371
The wind was blowing from the east.

64
00:03:03,391 --> 00:03:04,541
From the Soviet Union.

65
00:03:08,161 --> 00:03:09,330
A radioactive cloud

66
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:12,041
had spread over several European countries.

67
00:03:13,661 --> 00:03:15,501
The world was asking questions.

68
00:03:15,521 --> 00:03:17,621
But the Soviet Union stayed silent.

69
00:03:20,661 --> 00:03:21,784
Finally, that night,

70
00:03:21,804 --> 00:03:24,261
a twenty-second report aired on television.

71
00:03:26,561 --> 00:03:28,861
An accident has occurred. That was all.

72
00:03:33,611 --> 00:03:35,077
For the next two weeks,

73
00:03:35,097 --> 00:03:39,214
helicopters kept dropping sand, lead and boron into the reactor.

74
00:03:39,234 --> 00:03:40,591
Five thousand tonnes.

75
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:43,778
The machines that fought this battle

76
00:03:43,798 --> 00:03:44,858
became so radioactive

77
00:03:44,878 --> 00:03:46,165
they had to be abandoned.

78
00:03:49,861 --> 00:03:53,184
In September, debris had to be cleared from the roof of Unit Three.

79
00:03:53,204 --> 00:03:54,701
Machines could not work there.

80
00:03:55,278 --> 00:03:56,818
So men were sent instead.

81
00:03:56,838 --> 00:03:58,898
Each man had only ninety seconds.

82
00:04:00,695 --> 00:04:01,431
By November,

83
00:04:01,451 --> 00:04:04,327
a concrete tomb stood over the ruined reactor.

84
00:04:04,347 --> 00:04:05,355
The Sarcophagus.

85
00:04:09,445 --> 00:04:11,288
Around six hundred thousand people

86
00:04:11,308 --> 00:04:12,877
helped contain this disaster.

87
00:04:12,897 --> 00:04:14,705
They were called the liquidators.

88
00:04:16,528 --> 00:04:19,088
And Pripyat has stood empty ever since.

89
00:04:23,161 --> 00:04:24,228
Chunks of graphite

90
00:04:24,248 --> 00:04:26,601
lay scattered across the reactor roofs.

91
00:04:29,361 --> 00:04:31,145
The radiation there was so intense

92
00:04:31,165 --> 00:04:33,021
that even machines stopped working.

93
00:04:36,061 --> 00:04:38,061
Robots were brought in from abroad.

94
00:04:38,081 --> 00:04:40,101
They too broke down within minutes.

95
00:04:41,561 --> 00:04:43,901
In the end, the job had to be done by humans.

96
00:04:48,161 --> 00:04:50,041
They were called bio-robots.

97
00:04:53,661 --> 00:04:55,343
Each man could stay on the roof

98
00:04:55,363 --> 00:04:56,681
for just ninety seconds.

99
00:05:00,661 --> 00:05:02,623
Shovel a few pieces over the edge,

100
00:05:02,643 --> 00:05:03,543
and get back.

101
00:05:06,561 --> 00:05:07,257
By November,

102
00:05:07,277 --> 00:05:09,901
a concrete structure stood over the reactor.

103
00:05:13,611 --> 00:05:15,611
The world still had not been told.

104
00:05:16,528 --> 00:05:18,357
On the night of April twenty-eighth,

105
00:05:18,377 --> 00:05:21,048
Soviet television gave the news just twenty seconds.

106
00:05:22,361 --> 00:05:23,962
On the first of May, in Kyiv,

107
00:05:23,982 --> 00:05:26,161
the May Day parade marched on as usual.

108
00:05:27,361 --> 00:05:28,283
People did not know

109
00:05:28,303 --> 00:05:29,841
there was radiation in the air.

110
00:05:32,361 --> 00:05:35,112
Water had flooded the chambers beneath the reactor.

111
00:05:35,132 --> 00:05:36,742
If the molten core reached it,

112
00:05:36,762 --> 00:05:38,501
a second explosion could follow.

113
00:05:39,861 --> 00:05:42,521
Three divers went in and opened the valves.

114
00:05:43,195 --> 00:05:45,194
One hundred and sixteen thousand people

115
00:05:45,214 --> 00:05:47,595
were evacuated from the thirty-kilometre zone.

116
00:05:52,361 --> 00:05:53,101
On May tenth,

117
00:05:53,121 --> 00:05:55,341
the fire in the core finally went out.

118
00:05:58,195 --> 00:05:58,850
But by then,

119
00:05:58,870 --> 00:06:01,515
the radioactive cloud had spread across Europe.

120
00:06:07,328 --> 00:06:09,508
The firefighters were flown to Moscow.

121
00:06:13,528 --> 00:06:14,863
Hospital Number Six.

122
00:06:14,883 --> 00:06:17,728
A special hospital for radiation patients.

123
00:06:20,228 --> 00:06:22,028
The doctors tried everything.

124
00:06:22,048 --> 00:06:23,868
Even bone marrow transplants.

125
00:06:25,728 --> 00:06:28,368
But the radiation doses were far too high.

126
00:06:32,328 --> 00:06:34,168
One by one, they began to die.

127
00:06:37,828 --> 00:06:39,766
They were buried in zinc coffins

128
00:06:39,786 --> 00:06:40,948
at Mitino Cemetery.

129
00:06:44,828 --> 00:06:46,269
Because even their bodies

130
00:06:46,289 --> 00:06:47,808
were giving off radiation.

131
00:06:50,728 --> 00:06:52,249
They paid for saving others

132
00:06:52,269 --> 00:06:53,468
with their own lives.

133
00:06:57,778 --> 00:06:59,554
The pine forest beside the plant

134
00:06:59,574 --> 00:07:01,818
turned red from the radiation, and died.

135
00:07:02,778 --> 00:07:05,078
It was bulldozed and buried in the ground.

136
00:07:07,361 --> 00:07:08,830
In Pripyat's schools,

137
00:07:08,850 --> 00:07:11,261
books still lie open on the desks.

138
00:07:16,945 --> 00:07:18,628
In October nineteen eighty-six,

139
00:07:18,648 --> 00:07:20,441
right next to the ruined reactor,

140
00:07:20,461 --> 00:07:21,725
Unit One was restarted.

141
00:07:26,945 --> 00:07:29,695
In July nineteen eighty-seven, there was a trial.

142
00:07:29,715 --> 00:07:30,882
Three plant officials

143
00:07:30,902 --> 00:07:32,485
were sentenced to ten years.

144
00:07:37,361 --> 00:07:40,041
The lights of Pripyat never came on again.

145
00:07:47,778 --> 00:07:49,994
Some elderly people ignored the government.

146
00:07:50,014 --> 00:07:51,678
They returned to their villages.

147
00:07:54,028 --> 00:07:55,828
For them, this land was home.

148
00:07:59,861 --> 00:08:00,741
In the nineties,

149
00:08:00,761 --> 00:08:02,597
thyroid cancer spread among those

150
00:08:02,617 --> 00:08:04,641
who had been children in eighty-six.

151
00:08:06,528 --> 00:08:07,408
Radioactive iodine

152
00:08:07,428 --> 00:08:09,328
had reached their bodies through milk.

153
00:08:12,361 --> 00:08:14,787
October eleventh, nineteen ninety-one.

154
00:08:14,807 --> 00:08:17,961
A fire broke out in the turbine hall of Unit Two.

155
00:08:19,028 --> 00:08:20,668
That unit never ran again.

156
00:08:24,861 --> 00:08:27,217
In December, the Soviet Union collapsed.

157
00:08:27,237 --> 00:08:29,296
Now the zone was the responsibility

158
00:08:29,316 --> 00:08:30,861
of an independent Ukraine.

159
00:08:37,778 --> 00:08:40,098
Checkpoints were built around the zone.

160
00:08:40,118 --> 00:08:42,678
Two thousand six hundred square kilometres,

161
00:08:42,698 --> 00:08:43,598
sealed off.

162
00:08:44,861 --> 00:08:46,941
Only people with permits could enter.

163
00:08:49,861 --> 00:08:50,789
In the nineties,

164
00:08:50,809 --> 00:08:53,001
looters stripped Pripyat almost bare.

165
00:08:55,695 --> 00:08:57,875
Furniture, metal, even radiators.

166
00:08:57,895 --> 00:08:59,695
Much of it was radioactive.

167
00:09:02,361 --> 00:09:02,979
And slowly,

168
00:09:02,999 --> 00:09:05,261
the forest began to take the city back.

169
00:09:07,778 --> 00:09:09,826
The same road, the same bicycle.

170
00:09:09,846 --> 00:09:11,138
Now among the trees.

171
00:09:14,861 --> 00:09:17,926
Hidden in the forest was a secret Soviet radar.

172
00:09:17,946 --> 00:09:19,981
Duga. Now it too stands silent.

173
00:09:27,328 --> 00:09:29,400
Pripyat was founded in nineteen seventy

174
00:09:29,420 --> 00:09:30,708
for the plant's workers.

175
00:09:33,528 --> 00:09:34,751
It was a young city.

176
00:09:34,771 --> 00:09:37,008
The average age was just twenty-six.

177
00:09:40,228 --> 00:09:42,441
It had schools, a hospital, a cinema,

178
00:09:42,461 --> 00:09:43,668
and a swimming pool.

179
00:09:45,728 --> 00:09:47,226
People left for a few days.

180
00:09:47,246 --> 00:09:48,988
Their belongings stayed behind.

181
00:09:52,328 --> 00:09:53,698
Later, homes were looted.

182
00:09:53,718 --> 00:09:55,108
Everything was scattered.

183
00:09:57,828 --> 00:09:58,919
In the classrooms,

184
00:09:58,939 --> 00:10:00,728
books still lie on the desks.

185
00:10:04,828 --> 00:10:06,218
A city of fifty thousand

186
00:10:06,238 --> 00:10:07,648
emptied in a single day.

187
00:10:10,728 --> 00:10:11,848
And it never filled again.

188
00:10:17,778 --> 00:10:19,410
The hastily built Sarcophagus

189
00:10:19,430 --> 00:10:20,798
was starting to crumble.

190
00:10:22,361 --> 00:10:23,498
Its walls were cracked,

191
00:10:23,518 --> 00:10:25,581
and rainwater leaked in through the roof.

192
00:10:30,278 --> 00:10:30,972
One by one,

193
00:10:30,992 --> 00:10:33,180
the other reactors were shut down.

194
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:34,758
In ninety-six, Unit One.

195
00:10:42,361 --> 00:10:44,352
December fifteenth, two thousand.

196
00:10:44,372 --> 00:10:46,261
One seventeen in the afternoon.

197
00:10:47,778 --> 00:10:49,576
The last reactor, Unit Three,

198
00:10:49,596 --> 00:10:51,038
was shut down for good.

199
00:10:55,695 --> 00:10:57,079
After twenty-three years,

200
00:10:57,099 --> 00:10:58,895
the Chernobyl plant fell silent.

201
00:11:07,778 --> 00:11:09,718
Where people left, nature returned.

202
00:11:12,361 --> 00:11:15,661
In nineteen ninety-eight, rare wild horses were released here.

203
00:11:15,681 --> 00:11:17,501
Their herd is still growing today.

204
00:11:20,695 --> 00:11:23,315
Wolves, elk, deer, wild boar.

205
00:11:25,695 --> 00:11:26,412
Without humans,

206
00:11:26,432 --> 00:11:29,035
their numbers grew higher than in the forests around.

207
00:11:33,195 --> 00:11:34,316
Pripyat was slowly

208
00:11:34,336 --> 00:11:36,175
disappearing into the forest.

209
00:11:44,861 --> 00:11:46,432
The radiation is still here.

210
00:11:46,452 --> 00:11:47,852
But the absence of people

211
00:11:47,872 --> 00:11:50,201
was a far greater relief for the animals.

212
00:11:57,778 --> 00:11:58,815
The old Sarcophagus

213
00:11:58,835 --> 00:12:00,338
could not last much longer.

214
00:12:02,361 --> 00:12:04,029
Many countries came together

215
00:12:04,049 --> 00:12:06,341
to build a giant steel arch beside it.

216
00:12:09,861 --> 00:12:12,111
Two hundred and fifty-seven metres wide.

217
00:12:12,131 --> 00:12:14,061
One hundred and eight metres tall.

218
00:12:15,695 --> 00:12:18,135
The largest structure ever moved on land.

219
00:12:22,361 --> 00:12:24,039
In November twenty sixteen,

220
00:12:24,059 --> 00:12:26,701
it was slid on rails over the old reactor.

221
00:12:34,861 --> 00:12:36,651
This arch will protect the world

222
00:12:36,671 --> 00:12:38,821
from this reactor for a hundred years.

223
00:12:47,778 --> 00:12:49,838
But the price was paid by people.

224
00:12:51,528 --> 00:12:53,806
Firefighters, operators, liquidators.

225
00:12:53,826 --> 00:12:55,628
The ones who saved the world.

226
00:12:59,861 --> 00:13:02,692
In the first few weeks, thirty-one people died.

227
00:13:02,712 --> 00:13:04,329
They were buried in Moscow,

228
00:13:04,349 --> 00:13:05,441
in sealed coffins.

229
00:13:12,361 --> 00:13:14,699
About three hundred and fifty thousand people

230
00:13:14,719 --> 00:13:16,008
lost their homes forever.

231
00:13:16,028 --> 00:13:18,281
Hundreds of villages vanished from the map.

232
00:13:24,861 --> 00:13:26,715
How many lives were lost in total

233
00:13:26,735 --> 00:13:27,701
is still debated.

234
00:13:29,861 --> 00:13:30,843
From four thousand

235
00:13:30,863 --> 00:13:32,421
to more than sixty thousand.

236
00:13:37,778 --> 00:13:38,840
In twenty eleven,

237
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:40,898
the zone was opened to tourists.

238
00:13:43,195 --> 00:13:44,958
People come to see the Ferris wheel

239
00:13:44,978 --> 00:13:45,895
that never turned.

240
00:13:49,861 --> 00:13:51,270
The Azure swimming pool.

241
00:13:51,290 --> 00:13:53,234
For ten years after the disaster,

242
00:13:53,254 --> 00:13:54,921
liquidators still swam here.

243
00:14:02,361 --> 00:14:03,652
Beneath the reactor,

244
00:14:03,672 --> 00:14:06,010
a mass of melted fuel still remains.

245
00:14:06,030 --> 00:14:07,341
The Elephant's Foot.

246
00:14:09,861 --> 00:14:12,271
Once, standing near it for just a few minutes

247
00:14:12,291 --> 00:14:13,191
could kill you.

248
00:14:14,861 --> 00:14:15,820
In twenty nineteen,

249
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:18,621
one hundred and twenty-four thousand people came here.

250
00:14:27,328 --> 00:14:28,242
After Chernobyl,

251
00:14:28,262 --> 00:14:30,248
the world's nuclear rules changed.

252
00:14:33,528 --> 00:14:35,029
In other reactors like it,

253
00:14:35,049 --> 00:14:36,688
the design flaws were fixed.

254
00:14:40,228 --> 00:14:42,308
Today, many of them have been shut down.

255
00:14:45,728 --> 00:14:46,943
In twenty twenty-two,

256
00:14:46,963 --> 00:14:48,728
the zone was back in the news.

257
00:14:52,328 --> 00:14:54,484
During the war, soldiers dug trenches

258
00:14:54,504 --> 00:14:56,268
in the soil of the Red Forest.

259
00:14:57,828 --> 00:14:59,808
Five weeks later, they pulled out.

260
00:15:04,828 --> 00:15:06,828
Chernobyl is not just an accident.

261
00:15:10,728 --> 00:15:11,668
It is a reminder

262
00:15:11,688 --> 00:15:13,408
that the price of one mistake

263
00:15:13,428 --> 00:15:14,808
is paid by generations.

264
00:15:17,861 --> 00:15:18,221
Today,

265
00:15:18,241 --> 00:15:20,841
a giant steel arch stands over Chernobyl.

266
00:15:22,695 --> 00:15:24,057
Beneath it lies the reactor

267
00:15:24,077 --> 00:15:25,255
that changed the world.

268
00:15:30,195 --> 00:15:30,688
Pripyat.

269
00:15:30,708 --> 00:15:33,275
Once home to forty-nine thousand people.

270
00:15:34,861 --> 00:15:37,070
Today, there is only forest here.

271
00:15:37,090 --> 00:15:37,990
And silence.

272
00:15:42,028 --> 00:15:43,054
Parts of this land

273
00:15:43,074 --> 00:15:45,688
will stay radioactive for thousands of years.

274
00:15:46,695 --> 00:15:48,414
This story is in memory of all

275
00:15:48,434 --> 00:15:49,535
who paid the price.

